# Scroll-Driven 3D Disassembly (Exploded View)

An object separates into its components as the user scrolls, and reassembles on scroll-up. Contents:

1. [The math](#1-the-math)
2. [Vanilla Three.js implementation](#2-vanilla-threejs)
3. [React Three Fiber implementation](#3-react-three-fiber)
4. [Staged (sequenced) disassembly](#4-staged-disassembly)
5. [Interactivity: hover labels & part selection](#5-interactivity)
6. [The video alternative](#6-video-alternative)
7. [Pitfalls](#7-pitfalls)

Prerequisite: a GLB whose parts are separate named meshes, loaded with originals snapshotted — see `references/glb-assets.md` §7–8. The scroll scaffolding (pinned scene, Lenis, ScrollTrigger) is in `references/landing-pages.md`.

## 1. The math

For each part, precompute an **explode vector** — the direction it flies — then every frame set:

```
position = originalPosition + explodeVector × explodeAmount × easedProgress
```

Three ways to get explode vectors, best-first:

1. **Radial from assembly center, using world-space mesh centers** (default). Compute each part's bounding-box center in world space, subtract the whole model's center, normalize. Using bbox centers instead of `child.position` is the key correctness detail: many GLBs have all parts at local position `(0,0,0)` with the geometry offset baked into vertices — `position.clone().normalize()` on those yields a zero vector and nothing moves.
2. **Axis-projected** — project the radial vector onto one axis (e.g., Y) for a "layer cake" teardown of stacked devices. Looks more like a real product teardown than a spherical burst.
3. **Hand-authored** — a `{ partName: [x,y,z] }` map for hero moments where a specific part must fly a specific way. Combine: authored for the 3 hero parts, radial for the rest.

Ease the progress (e.g., `power2.inOut`) rather than feeding raw scroll linearly — linear explosion reads mechanical and cheap.

## 2. Vanilla Three.js

Complete pattern, assuming the loader from glb-assets §7 already ran (originals snapshotted, model centered):

```javascript
// --- Precompute explode vectors once, after load ---
const modelCenter = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(model).getCenter(new THREE.Vector3());
const parts = [];
model.updateWorldMatrix(true, true);
model.traverse((child) => {
  if (!child.isMesh) return;
  const partCenter = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(child).getCenter(new THREE.Vector3());
  let dir = partCenter.sub(modelCenter);
  if (dir.lengthSq() < 1e-6) dir.set(0, 1, 0);      // dead-center part: push it up
  // Optional "layer cake": dir.multiply(new THREE.Vector3(0.25, 1, 0.25));
  parts.push({ mesh: child, dir: dir.normalize() });
});

// --- Drive from scroll ---
const state = { progress: 0 };
gsap.to(state, {
  progress: 1, ease: 'none',
  scrollTrigger: { trigger: '.disassembly-scene', start: 'top top', end: 'bottom bottom', scrub: 0.5 }
});

const EXPLODE = 2.5;                                 // world units at full explode
function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  const eased = gsap.parseEase('power2.inOut')(state.progress);
  for (const { mesh, dir } of parts) {
    mesh.position.copy(mesh.userData.originalPosition).addScaledVector(dir, eased * EXPLODE);
    mesh.rotation.y = eased * 0.35;                  // slight spin sells the motion
  }
  model.rotation.y = state.progress * Math.PI * 0.5; // whole-model turn across the scene
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
animate();
```

Two structural rules embodied above:

- **Always compute from `originalPosition`, never increment the live position.** Incremental updates accumulate float error and depend on frame rate; scrubbing backward would never return parts exactly home.
- **Scroll writes a number; the render loop reads it.** Decoupling scroll events from rendering keeps the animation smooth even when scroll events burst or stall.

Canvas-in-page setup: renderer canvas `position: fixed/sticky; top:0; inset:0` inside the pinned stage, `renderer.setSize` + `camera.aspect` updated on resize, `renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(devicePixelRatio, 2))` (full retina DPR on a fullscreen canvas halves the frame rate for invisible gain).

## 3. React Three Fiber

drei's `ScrollControls` replaces GSAP's ScrollTrigger inside the canvas world:

```tsx
import { Canvas, useFrame } from '@react-three/fiber';
import { useGLTF, useScroll, ScrollControls, Environment, Html } from '@react-three/drei';
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { useMemo, useRef } from 'react';
import { easing } from 'maath';

function ExplodingModel({ url }: { url: string }) {
  const { scene } = useGLTF(url);
  const scroll = useScroll();

  const parts = useMemo(() => {
    scene.updateWorldMatrix(true, true);
    const center = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(scene).getCenter(new THREE.Vector3());
    const list: { mesh: THREE.Mesh; dir: THREE.Vector3; home: THREE.Vector3 }[] = [];
    scene.traverse((child) => {
      if ((child as THREE.Mesh).isMesh) {
        const m = child as THREE.Mesh;
        const c = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(m).getCenter(new THREE.Vector3());
        const dir = c.sub(center);
        if (dir.lengthSq() < 1e-6) dir.set(0, 1, 0);
        list.push({ mesh: m, dir: dir.normalize(), home: m.position.clone() });
      }
    });
    return list;
  }, [scene]);

  useFrame((_, delta) => {
    const t = THREE.MathUtils.smoothstep(scroll.offset, 0, 1); // eased 0..1
    for (const { mesh, dir, home } of parts) {
      const target = home.clone().addScaledVector(dir, t * 2.5);
      easing.damp3(mesh.position, target, 0.12, delta);        // spring-smoothed
    }
    scene.rotation.y = scroll.offset * Math.PI * 0.5;
  });

  return <primitive object={scene} />;
}

export default function App() {
  return (
    <Canvas camera={{ position: [0, 1.5, 6], fov: 45 }} dpr={[1, 2]}>
      <Environment preset="studio" />
      <ScrollControls pages={4} damping={0.15}>
        <ExplodingModel url="/model.glb" />
      </ScrollControls>
    </Canvas>
  );
}
```

`pages={4}` ≈ the 400vh spacer from the vanilla version. If the 3D canvas must live *inside* a larger GSAP-choreographed page rather than own the scroll, skip `ScrollControls` and pass ScrollTrigger's progress into the component via a ref or zustand store instead — mixing two scroll-hijacking systems on one page causes fights.

## 4. Staged disassembly

Real teardowns are sequenced: lid first, then board, then battery. Give each part a stage window and remap progress per part:

```javascript
const STAGES = { top_case: [0.00, 0.30], screen: [0.15, 0.50], board: [0.40, 0.75], battery: [0.60, 1.00] };

function stageProgress(global, [a, b]) {
  return THREE.MathUtils.smoothstep(global, a, b);   // 0 before a, 1 after b, eased between
}
// per frame:  local = stageProgress(state.progress, STAGES[mesh.name] ?? [0, 1]);
```

Overlapping windows (`screen` starts before `top_case` finishes) keep motion continuous. Pair each stage with a synced DOM caption using labeled ScrollTrigger positions on the same scene.

## 5. Interactivity

The reason to choose real-time over video — use it:

- **Hover highlight:** raycast on pointermove (R3F: `onPointerOver` on `<primitive>` children); on hit, lerp the part's material `emissive` toward an accent color, show a label. Clone materials per part on load first — GLB parts often share one material instance, and tinting a shared material tints everything.
- **Labels:** drei `<Html position={...} occlude>` pinned to part centers, or project part centers to screen space (`vector.project(camera)`) and position absolutely-placed DOM chips. Fade labels in only when explode progress > ~0.6 so the assembled view stays clean.
- **Click to isolate:** on part click, tween all other parts' material opacity to 0.08 (`transparent: true` required) and dolly the camera toward the part's center; click background to restore.

## 6. Video alternative

When fidelity beats interactivity, render the disassembly offline (Blender: animate parts along their explode vectors, keyframe 0→100%; or the user's motion-graphics footage) and scrub it — full pattern and the critical all-keyframe ffmpeg encode in `references/landing-pages.md` §7. Sync DOM captions to the same ScrollTrigger driving `currentTime` so text lands on the right frames.

## 7. Pitfalls

- **`child.position.clone().normalize()` as the explode direction** — breaks on GLBs with baked-in vertex offsets (direction is zero) and on parts dead-center (NaN after normalize). Use world bbox centers with the zero-length guard, as above.
- **Traversing the scene graph every frame** — `traverse` allocates and walks the whole tree; precompute the flat `parts` array once.
- **Forgetting `updateWorldMatrix(true, true)` before measuring** — bounding boxes computed before the first render use identity matrices and give wrong centers.
- **Shared materials** — highlighting one part highlights five. Clone on load.
- **Nested part hierarchies** — if parts are nested groups (screws parented to the lid), decide the granularity: either explode only top-level children of the model root (keeps sub-assemblies together — usually what you want), or flatten with `SkeletonUtils`/reparenting first. Exploding every descendant mesh of a nested hierarchy double-applies motion (parent moves + child moves).
- **No reduced-motion path** — offer a static exploded illustration (set progress to a fixed 0.7) instead of the scroll animation when `prefers-reduced-motion` is set.
